From a1ec12cb6fe823899c4100b2ba3f512874f7dcd0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 06:09:18 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Re-arm the time-to-first-fix measurement when a position
 session ends

getTimeToFirstFix measures the right thing but only ever once. mTTFFState
latches on the first fix and was never cleared, so mFixRequestTime and
mLastFixTime froze for the lifetime of the process and every later call
returned the same stale number.

Measured on device. A cold first fix outdoors took 245100ms by wall clock and
reported 244492ms, which is accurate to the resolution of the measurement. A
second session two minutes later, warm, took 2010ms - and still reported
244492ms. Stopping GPS in between did not help, because STOP_POSITION_CMD
cleared LOCATION_UPDATES_ON and shut the engine down without touching the TTFF
state.

Time to first fix is a property of a session, so reset it when the session
ends. This also matters for anything trying to compare cold and warm starts:
before this, the second and every subsequent measurement in a process was
silently the first one.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [LuneOS specific]
---
 src/handler/position/gps/GPSPositionProvider.cpp | 16 ++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/handler/position/gps/GPSPositionProvider.cpp b/src/handler/position/gps/GPSPositionProvider.cpp
index 53f24c3..8211595 100644
--- a/src/handler/position/gps/GPSPositionProvider.cpp
+++ b/src/handler/position/gps/GPSPositionProvider.cpp
@@ -947,6 +947,22 @@ ErrorCodes GPSPositionProvider::processRequest(PositionRequest request) {
                 mPosTimer = 0;
             }
             mAPIProgressFlag &= ~LOCATION_UPDATES_ON;
+
+            /*
+             * Re-arm the time-to-first-fix measurement for the next session.
+             *
+             * mTTFFState latches on the first fix and was never cleared, so
+             * both timestamps froze for the lifetime of the process and every
+             * later getTimeToFirstFix returned the same stale number. Measured
+             * on device: a first fix took 245s and reported 244492ms, correctly;
+             * a second session two minutes later took 2010ms and still reported
+             * 244492ms. TTFF is per session, so it has to reset when the
+             * session ends.
+             */
+            mTTFFState = false;
+            mFixRequestTime = 0;
+            mLastFixTime = 0;
+            mTTFF = 0;
             if (!mAPIProgressFlag) {
                 if (mEngineStarted) {
                     if (false == processCommand(COMMAND_GPS_DISABLE)) {
